{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>A half-shekel of the sacred shekel.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): since Moses was king of Israel, as it is written ״ויהי בישרון מלך״, he therefore established for himself a silver coin and called it a shekel, because the entire coin was of full weight, for the silver was refined without dross. And he called it sacred (קדש) because all the commandments are the essence of holiness, and in some commandments there is need for that coin, such as the commandment of redeeming the firstborn son, where the obligation is five shekels; also the commandment of valuations (ערכין), which are called קדש, requires shekels. For this reason we call the language of our Torah לשון הקדש (the sacred tongue), because it is a language of holiness, encompassing many kinds of holiness, and all matters of holiness employ it — for it is the language in which the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke with Israel the Ten Commandments, and in which He speaks with the prophets and with the angels. And the seventy Names are all in לשון הקדש; also the names of the angels are called in לשון הקדש, as for example גבריאל is called after גבורה (might), and רפאל from רפואה (healing); and many of them are sealed with the name אל, to tell you that His name is shared among them, and this is what is written (Psalms 68): ״רכב אלהים רבותים אלפי שנאן ה' בם״, and so all of them. And it is the language in which the world was created, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: the world was created in לשון הקדש, as it is said (Genesis 2): ״לזאת יקרא אשה כי מאיש לקחה זאת״, and the proof is from the naming of names — אדם, חוה, קין, הבל — all of them לשון הקודש, and so all the rest of the names. And it is the language to which all the nations and all the languages are destined to return, as it is said (Zephaniah 3): ״כי אז אהפוך אל עמים שפה ברורה לקרא כלם בשם ה' לעבדו שכם אחד״. And the reason it was a half-shekel is because Israel transgressed in the matter of the calf against the Ten Commandments, and a half-shekel is ten gerah, since a whole shekel is twenty gerah; therefore each and every one of Israel would bring ten gerah, to serve them as atonement for having transgressed the Ten Commandments. And none would bring except one of twenty years and above, because the heavenly court does not punish at less than twenty. And likewise the half-shekel was one tenth of the redemption of the firstborn, which is five shekels, as it is said (Numbers 18:15–16): ״ופדויו מבן חדש תפדה בערכך כסף חמשת שקלים״ and so forth." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> a half-shekel — because they sinned at midday, therefore they shall give a half-shekel. This is what is written (Exodus 32): ״כי בשש משה בא שש״. The word שש alludes both to the sixth hour and to shesh (six)." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> it was a half-shekel, though it could have been a whole shekel of ten gerah; but it comes to hint that it is fitting for every thoughtful person to weigh his deeds equally, and to apportion for himself, for his body and for his soul, to each of them its portion and its due in the full weight. The proper and fitting course is that he draw his body toward the service of HaShem, blessed be He, and follow therein after his soul, which is the essence; and at times also that he draw his soul after his body in necessary matters, such as eating and drinking and clothing, without which he cannot live — as our Rabbis of blessed memory said regarding the matter of the festival: it is written (Deuteronomy 16) ״עצרת לה' אלהיך״, and it is written (Numbers 29) ״עצרת תהיה לכם״; Rabbi Yehoshua said: divide it — half for HaShem and half for yourselves. And it is fitting that his intention in striving for the needs of his body be so that benefit to the soul may come from it, as we find with our father Jacob, who engaged in the affairs of the soul for fourteen years, serving in the house of Eber, and engaged in the affairs of the body in physical labor for fourteen years as well, with the two daughters of Laban; and the ultimate purpose of his intention in physical labor was for the benefit of the intellectual soul. The half-shekel should be לשם ה' (for the Name of HaShem), for one should weigh all his deeds and actions and divide them in a balanced way, to each one what is fitting for it. And the verse mentions שקל הקדש, for then all the shekels of his deeds and his conduct are weighed in the שקל קדש, and from this he ascends to the rungs of abstention and holiness." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> a half-shekel — according to what they devised in their thought to set a cutting and a barrier (חציצה) between the fire and the voice at Mount Sinai, therefore they needed to bring a half-shekel to serve as atonement for their sin. And since a cutting (קצוץ) in one of the ten is called קצוץ, therefore they needed to bring ten gerah in the half-shekel and not in a whole shekel. And since they split (מבקעים) in unity, therefore this silver is called בקע. And because they made a head from the end and an upper one from the abyss that lies beneath, therefore from this silver were made the sockets (אדנים) set in the Tabernacle, so that the Tabernacle would rest upon them — in order that the opposite of their thought be made manifest from this. And when they were weighed in the balance of the Great Name, which is the whole sacred shekel, they were found lacking, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 5): ״תקל תקלתא במאזניא והשתכחת חסיר״. And because this is the great sin and their punishment reached to the soul, therefore they were obligated to give a half-shekel — ״זה תרומה לה'״ — and it is ״כופר לנפש״ (an atonement for the soul)." "<b>A terumah to HaShem.</b> In this section the word תרומה appears three times, to teach that there are three terumot: the terumah of the Tabernacle, the terumah of the shekels, and the terumah of the altar. And I already mentioned this in the portion ויקחו לי תרומה, and there I expanded upon the explanation. And the terumah of the shekels specified here — from it were made the sockets (אדנים), for this silver amounted to a hundred talents, as it is written in אלה פקודי: ״וכסף פקודי העדה מאת ככר״, and it all went into the sockets of the Tabernacle. And the sockets of the Tabernacle were one hundred, with one talent per socket, as it is written (Exodus 38:27): ״ככר לאדן״. And how were the sockets one hundred? The boards (קרשים) were twenty on the north side and twenty on the south side; and it is written (ibid. v. 26): ״והיו שמונה קרשים״ for the west side — that gives you forty-eight boards. On the east side there were no boards at all. Assign two sockets per board, as it is written: ״שני אדנים תחת הקרש״ — that gives you ninety-six sockets. And on the east side there were four sockets on which the veil (פרוכת) was suspended — that makes a hundred sockets that were in the Tabernacle; and these are what were made from this terumah of the shekels."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The citation of the verse about Moses as king alludes to Deuteronomy 33:5 (ויהי בישרון מלך); the author quotes only the opening words.", 3 "The Kabbalah passage is rendered hyper-literally throughout, preserving the cryptic allusions to severing divine unities (the 'ten,' the 'fire and voice') and to the inverted spiritual hierarchy ('head from the end,' 'upper from the abyss') exactly as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:58.824892564Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}